<h2 id="90-day-plan">A 90-day plan to start earning</h2>
<p>If you want both income streams, here is a realistic sequence for someone working 5–10 hours a week:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Days 1–30: Learn and set up.</strong> Finish one Semrush Academy SEO course and its exam, start a free account, and pick a niche. Set up a simple site or blog where you will publish tutorials, and apply to the affiliate program once a few posts are live.</li>
  <li><strong>Days 31–60: Sell one service.</strong> Launch a single keyword research or audit offer on a gig marketplace and pitch five local businesses directly. Do the first few projects at a modest price in exchange for testimonials and permission to share results.</li>
  <li><strong>Days 61–90: Document and publish.</strong> Turn each project into a tutorial or case study on your site, with affiliate links where the tool genuinely helped. Raise your service prices as reviews come in.</li>
</ul>
<p>By the end of three months you will not have passive income yet, since SEO content takes time to rank, but you should have paying clients, proof of results and a growing library of content that can start earning commissions. A simple [[amazon:planner|side hustle planner]] helps keep the weekly hours honest.</p>

<h2 id="mistakes">Mistakes Semrush affiliates make</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Promoting to the wrong audience.</strong> A general lifestyle audience rarely needs a professional SEO tool. Semrush converts best with marketers, bloggers, agency owners, store owners and freelancers.</li>
  <li><strong>Writing reviews without using the tool.</strong> Readers can tell. Screenshots from your own projects, real limitations and specific use cases build the trust that turns a click into a trial.</li>
  <li><strong>Hiding the disclosure.</strong> The FTC expects a clear disclosure near affiliate links. It does not hurt conversions as much as people fear, and it protects your account.</li>
  <li><strong>Counting on trials alone.</strong> Trial payouts are nice, but sales are where the money is. Content that helps readers get value during the trial window converts more of them into paying subscribers.</li>
  <li><strong>Ignoring other languages.</strong> The program is global. If you write well in Spanish, French or German, those SERPs are often less competitive than English for the same topics.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="taxes">Taxes on affiliate and consulting income</h2>
<p>Affiliate commissions and consulting fees are self-employment income in the US. Impact collects tax information during signup, and you owe tax on what you earn whether or not you receive a 1099. Set aside a portion of each payout for income and self-employment tax; the <a href="/tools/self-employment-tax-calculator/">self-employment tax calculator</a> gives you a number. Your Semrush subscription, courses and software are typically deductible business expenses.</p>

<h2 id="bottom-line">The bottom line</h2>
<p>The Semrush affiliate program is one of the best-paying software programs for anyone with a marketing-minded audience, thanks to flat $200–$450 sale commissions and a 120-day cookie. If you are starting from zero, build skills first: use Semrush Academy, sell a narrow SEO service, and let real client results become your best affiliate content. That is the path Better Side Hustles recommends for anyone who wants SEO income that lasts.</p>
